    question: "Inheritance vs Composition",
    slug: "deloitte-inheritance-vs-composition-52",
    shortDescription: "Compare inheritance and composition.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nInheritance models an is-a relationship and shares behavior through a type hierarchy; composition builds behavior by holding other objects. Composition is often easier to change because dependencies are explicit.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you understand the design or language idea well enough to use it, not just repeat its definition. The interviewer can probe coupling, maintainability, runtime behavior, or language constraints.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the concept in one sentence.\n2. Explain the language or design rule behind it.\n3. Show the consequence with a small example.\n4. State the main trade-off or limitation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define the concept first, then explain why the language or design uses that model. I would give one concrete example and finish with the limitation I would keep in mind in production.\n\n**Example :**\nInstead of subclassing NotificationService for every channel, compose it from a Sender interface and inject an EmailSender or SmsSender.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nComposition is generally preferable when behavior may vary independently; inheritance is useful when a genuine subtype relationship and substitutability exist.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing a language feature with a design principle.\n- Giving a textbook definition without explaining the consequence in code.\n- Presenting a guideline as an absolute rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
